          {
            title: 'Quebec Rummy',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Quebec Rummy (Rami QuÃ©bÃ©cois) is a regional variation of Contract Rummy played extensively in French-speaking Canada. It is characterized by its specific sequence of 7 or 11 rounds of 'Contracts' and its unique rules regarding 'Buying' from the discard pile (The 'Pige'). Like other contract variants, players must fulfill exactly what the round calls forâ€”sets or runsâ€”before they can expand their melds. It is a social game often played at family dinners (les grandes tablÃ©es) with multiple decks to accommodate large groups.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Derived from Continental Rummy and early 20th-century American contract variants. It evolved within Quebecois social clubs and homes into a standardized series of 'Steps'.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Optimal at 4-6 players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2 standard decks + 4 Jokers.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2 standard decks + 4 Jokers.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '11 cards each player every round.',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
