          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: "Complete the round's contract and be the first to reach zero cards. Lowest aggregate score wins.",
          gameplay: 'Players play 7 or 11 rounds. Each round has a contract (specific sets and runs). Each turn: draw one from stock or buy from discard (La Pige; 2-card penalty), fulfill the round contract then expand melds, discard one card. First to empty hand wins the round; remaining hands are scored. Lowest aggregate score after all rounds wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Each round has a contract (sets and runs); fulfill it before expanding melds.',
            'Draw one from stock or buy from discard (La Pige; then draw two extra from stock).',
            'Meld and lay off on any meld; discard one card to end turn.',
            'First to empty hand wins the round; lowest aggregate score after all rounds wins.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Collect 'Connectors' for runs early. In Quebec Rummy, runs are more common and flexible than sets. If you have 6 and 8 of Spades, keep them bothâ€”drawing any Spade 5, 7, or 9 immediately builds a path toward a contract. Don't 'Pige' (buy) just for a single card unless it *directly* finishes your contractâ€”the 2-card draw penalty is a heavy burden that will make it harder to go out later.",
          intermediate: "The 'Pige' Bluff: If you see an opponent has only 3 cards left, they are close to going out. If a card is discarded that *might* help them, even if you don't need it, 'Pige' it to take it away. You are paying a 2-card penalty to stop them from winning. This is a defensive trade-off that is essential in the late-game rounds.",
          advanced: "Joker Liberation Management: Never use your Joker in a place where an opponent can easily liberate it. If you put a Joker in a Diamond Run of 5-6-Joker-8, someone with the Diamond 7 will steal your Joker and win the game. Instead, use Jokers in 'High-Point' sets (Kings or Aces) where the natural cards are harder for others to spare.",
          tips: [
            "If you have already laid down your contract, you can 'Play on' anyone's cards. Dump your high-value cards on the table as fast as possible.",
            'The final round (Three Runs) is the hardestâ€”save your Jokers for this round even if it means taking a few extra points in earlier rounds.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
